            { title: "UNION removes duplicates, UNION ALL keeps them", body: "**UNION** stacks and **removes duplicates**: each distinct row appears once. **UNION ALL** stacks and **removes nothing**: every row from both sets appears, including rows present in both.\n\nChoose by the prompt's intent: each distinct row once means UNION; every occurrence is real data that must be counted means UNION ALL. Swapping them is the classic error in both directions: UNION silently deletes duplicate rows the count needed, and UNION ALL keeps duplicates the prompt wanted collapsed.\n\nNot covered by the held decks: the stacking requirements for the two result sets (how many columns, of what types) are **not stated in the Lecture 1 to 4 decks**, and the decks work **no UNION query** on any table. Both are flagged in this topic's provisional note; verify before relying on them." }
